<h2 id="-1-linux-fundamentals">🐧 1. Linux Fundamentals</h2>

<p>Before becoming a Linux Administrator, it's important to understand the basic concepts of Linux and build a proper lab environment.</p>

<h3>Lab Setup</h3>
<p>Practice Linux inside a virtual environment before working on production servers.</p>
<p><b>Recommended options:</b></p>
<ul>
  <li>Oracle VirtualBox</li>
  <li>VMware Workstation</li>
  <li>KVM</li>
</ul>

<h3>Popular Linux Distributions</h3>
<table>
  <thead>
    <tr>
      <th>Distribution</th>
      <th>Purpose</th>
    </tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr><td>Ubuntu</td><td>Beginner Friendly</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Debian</td><td>Stable Servers</td></tr>
    <tr><td>RHEL</td><td>Enterprise Production</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Rocky Linux</td><td>Free RHEL Alternative</td></tr>
    <tr><td>AlmaLinux</td><td>Enterprise Linux</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Kali Linux</td><td>Penetration Testing</td></tr>
  </tbody>
</table>

<h3>Linux File System</h3>
<p>Understanding the Linux directory structure is essential.</p>
<pre><code>/
├── bin
├── boot
├── dev
├── etc
├── home
├── lib
├── media
├── mnt
├── opt
├── proc
├── root
├── run
├── srv
├── sys
├── tmp
├── usr
└── var</code></pre>

<h3>Essential Commands</h3>
<pre><code>pwd  ls  cd  mkdir  rm  cp  mv  touch  cat  less  nano  vim  find  locate  grep  echo  history  clear</code></pre>

<hr>

<h2 id="-2-core-linux-administration">⚙️ 2. Core Linux Administration</h2>

<p>Linux Administrators spend most of their time managing users, files, software, and running processes.</p>

<h3>User Management Tasks</h3>
<ul>
  <li>Create Users</li>
  <li>Delete Users</li>
  <li>Modify Users</li>
  <li>Manage Groups</li>
  <li>Reset Passwords</li>
</ul>

<p><b>Commands:</b> <code>useradd</code>, <code>adduser</code>, <code>passwd</code>, <code>usermod</code>, <code>groupadd</code>, <code>groupdel</code>, <code>id</code>, <code>whoami</code></p>

<h3>File Permissions</h3>
<p>Linux permissions protect files and directories from unauthorized access.</p>
<p><b>Permission Types:</b> Read (r), Write (w), Execute (x)</p>
<p><b>Permission Categories:</b> Owner, Group, Others</p>

<p><b>Examples:</b></p>
<pre><code>chmod 755 file.txt
chmod 644 file.txt
chown user:user file.txt</code></pre>

<h3>Process Management</h3>
<p><b>Useful Commands:</b> <code>ps</code>, <code>top</code>, <code>htop</code>, <code>kill</code>, <code>killall</code>, <code>pkill</code>, <code>nice</code>, <code>renice</code></p>

<h3>Package Management</h3>
<table>
  <thead>
    <tr>
      <th>Distribution</th>
      <th>Package Manager</th>
    </tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr><td>Ubuntu</td><td>apt</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Debian</td><td>apt</td></tr>
    <tr><td>RHEL</td><td>yum</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Rocky Linux</td><td>dnf</td></tr>
    <tr><td>AlmaLinux</td><td>dnf</td></tr>
  </tbody>
</table>

<p><b>Examples:</b></p>
<pre><code>sudo apt update
sudo apt install nginx
sudo dnf install httpd
sudo yum install httpd</code></pre>

<hr>

<h2 id="-3-networking--security">🌐 3. Networking & Security</h2>

<h3>IP Configuration</h3>
<pre><code>ip a
ip route
hostnamectl
nmcli</code></pre>

<h3>Network Troubleshooting</h3>
<pre><code>ping
traceroute
netstat
ss
curl
wget
dig
nslookup</code></pre>

<h3>SSH</h3>
<pre><code>sudo systemctl start ssh
sudo systemctl enable ssh
ssh user@server-ip</code></pre>

<h3>Firewall</h3>
<p><b>Popular Firewalls:</b> UFW, Firewalld, iptables</p>
<pre><code>sudo ufw allow 22
sudo ufw enable
sudo firewall-cmd --list-all</code></pre>

<hr>

<h2 id="-4-monitoring--maintenance">📊 4. Monitoring & Maintenance</h2>

<h3>Monitor System Resources</h3>
<pre><code>top
htop
free -h
df -h
du -sh
uptime</code></pre>

<h3>Log Management</h3>
<p><b>Important Log Directory:</b> <code>/var/log/</code></p>
<pre><code>journalctl
tail
less
cat</code></pre>

<h3>Backup & Restore</h3>
<p><b>Common Backup Tools:</b> <code>tar</code>, <code>rsync</code>, <code>cp</code>, snapshots</p>
<pre><code>tar -czvf backup.tar.gz project/
rsync -av source destination</code></pre>

<h3>Cron Jobs</h3>
<p>Cron automates repetitive tasks.</p>
<pre><code>crontab -e</code></pre>
<p>Schedule Example (Runs every day at 2:00 AM):</p>
<pre><code>0 2 * * * backup.sh</code></pre>

<hr>

<h2 id="-5-advanced-administration--automation">🚀 5. Advanced Administration & Automation</h2>

<h3>Shell Scripting</h3>
<pre><code>#!/bin/bash
echo "System Health"
df -h
free -h</code></pre>

<h3>Storage Management</h3>
<details>
  <summary><b>LVM (Logical Volume Manager)</b></summary>
  <p>Allows flexible storage allocation. Advantages: Resize partitions, snapshot support, flexible storage.</p>
</details>

<br>

<details>
  <summary><b>RAID (Redundant Array of Independent Disks)</b></summary>
  <p>Provides redundancy, performance, and fault tolerance. Common Levels: RAID 0, RAID 1, RAID 5, RAID 6, RAID 10.</p>
</details>

<h3>Configuration Management</h3>
<p><b>Ansible:</b> Automates server configuration, package installation, user management, and service management.</p>

<h3>Virtualization & Containers</h3>
<ul>
  <li><b>Hypervisors:</b> KVM, VMware, VirtualBox</li>
  <li><b>Containers:</b> Docker, Podman (Lightweight, fast deployment, isolated)</li>
</ul>

<h3>High Availability & Load Balancing</h3>
<ul>
  <li><b>HA Tools:</b> Keepalived, Pacemaker</li>
  <li><b>Load Balancers:</b> Nginx, HAProxy</li>
  <li><b>Cloud Providers:</b> AWS, Azure, GCP</li>
</ul>

<hr>

<h2 id="-6-career-roadmap">💼 6. Career Roadmap</h2>

<h3>Recommended Certifications</h3>
<p>RHCSA, RHCE, Linux+, LPIC-1, LFCS</p>

<h3>Project Ideas</h3>
<ul>
  <li>Linux Home Server</li>
  <li>Apache / Nginx Web Server</li>
  <li>FTP / SSH / Samba File Server</li>
  <li>Docker / Kubernetes Lab</li>
  <li>Monitoring Server & SIEM Lab using Wazuh</li>
</ul>

