<hr>

<h2 id="-interview-questions">💼 Interview Questions</h2>

<details>
  <summary><b>Q1. What is the role of a Linux Administrator?</b></summary>
  <p><b>Answer:</b> A Linux Administrator installs, configures, secures, monitors, and maintains Linux servers while ensuring high availability and performance.</p>
</details>
<br>
<details>
  <summary><b>Q2. What is the difference between chmod and chown?</b></summary>
  <table>
    <tr><th>Command</th><th>Purpose</th></tr>
    <tr><td><code>chmod</code></td><td>Changes file permissions</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>chown</code></td><td>Changes file ownership</td></tr>
  </table>
</details>
<br>
<details>
  <summary><b>Q3. Which command is used to monitor running processes?</b></summary>
  <p><b>Answer:</b> <code>top</code>, <code>htop</code>, <code>ps</code></p>
</details>
<br>
<details>
  <summary><b>Q4. Why is SSH important?</b></summary>
  <p><b>Answer:</b> SSH provides secure encrypted remote access for managing Linux servers.</p>
</details>

<blockquote>
  <b>⭐ Pro Tip:</b> The best way to become a Linux Administrator is to build hands-on labs. Practice on Ubuntu, Rocky Linux, or RHEL using VirtualBox or VMware, automate tasks with Bash and Ansible, deploy applications with Docker, and document every project on GitHub!
</blockquote>

<hr>
<hr>

<h1 id="-linux-user-account-management">👤 Linux User Account Management</h1>

<p>A comprehensive guide to managing <b>users, groups, passwords, and account security</b> in Linux.</p>

<hr>

<h2>👤 1. User Management</h2>

<details open>
  <summary><b>➕ useradd (Create User)</b></summary>
  <br>
  <p>Syntax: <code>useradd username</code></p>
  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr><th>Option</th><th>Purpose</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td><code>-g</code></td><td>Assign a primary group</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>-s</code></td><td>Specify the default shell</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>-c</code></td><td>Add a comment or description</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>-m</code></td><td>Create the user's home directory</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>-d</code></td><td>Specify a custom home directory</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <br>
  <p><b>Examples:</b></p>
  <pre><code>sudo useradd john
sudo useradd -m john
sudo useradd -g developers john
sudo useradd -s /bin/bash john
sudo useradd -c "Web Administrator" john
sudo useradd -d /home/webadmin john</code></pre>
</details>

<br>

<details open>
  <summary><b>❌ userdel (Delete User)</b></summary>
  <br>
  <p>Syntax: <code>userdel username</code></p>
  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr><th>Option</th><th>Purpose</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td><code>-r</code></td><td>Delete the user and home directory</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>-f</code></td><td>Force delete the user even if logged in</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <br>
  <p><b>Examples:</b></p>
  <pre><code>sudo userdel john
sudo userdel -r john
sudo userdel -f john</code></pre>
</details>

<br>

<details open>
  <summary><b>✏️ usermod (Modify User)</b></summary>
  <br>
  <p>Modifies an existing user account (groups, shell, home directory, lock/unlock).</p>
  <pre><code>sudo usermod username</code></pre>
</details>

<hr>

<h2>👥 2. Group Management</h2>

<table>
  <thead>
    <tr><th>Action</th><th>Command</th><th>Syntax</th></tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr><td>Create Group</td><td><code>groupadd</code></td><td><code>sudo groupadd developers</code></td></tr>
    <tr><td>Delete Group</td><td><code>groupdel</code></td><td><code>sudo groupdel developers</code></td></tr>
  </tbody>
</table>

<hr>

<h2>🔐 3. Password & Account Security</h2>

<table>
  <thead>
    <tr><th>Task</th><th>Command</th></tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr><td>Set/Change Password</td><td><code>sudo passwd john</code></td></tr>
    <tr><td>Lock Account</td><td><code>sudo usermod -L john</code></td></tr>
    <tr><td>Unlock Account</td><td><code>sudo usermod -U john</code></td></tr>
  </tbody>
</table>

<hr>

<h2>📂 4. Important System Files</h2>

<table>
  <thead>
    <tr><th>File Path</th><th>Purpose / Description</th></tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr>
      <td><code>/etc/passwd</code></td>
      <td>Stores user account information (Username, UID, GID, Home Directory, Shell).</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><code>/etc/group</code></td>
      <td>Stores group definitions, GIDs, and group members.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><code>/etc/shadow</code></td>
      <td>Stores encrypted passwords and password policies. <i>(Accessible only by root)</i></td>
    </tr>
  </tbody>
</table>

<hr>

<h2>🔍 5. Useful Verification Commands</h2>

<table>
  <thead>
    <tr><th>Command</th><th>Purpose</th></tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr><td><code>id &lt;username&gt;</code></td><td>Display user details (UID, GID, Groups)</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>whoami</code></td><td>Show current logged-in user</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>id</code></td><td>Display current user ID details</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>who</code></td><td>List logged-in users</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>last</code></td><td>Display user login history</td></tr>
  </tbody>
</table>

<hr>

<h2>💼 Account Management Interview Questions</h2>

<details>
  <summary><b>Q1. Difference between /etc/passwd and /etc/shadow?</b></summary>
  <br>
  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr><th>/etc/passwd</th><th>/etc/shadow</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>Stores user account details</td><td>Stores encrypted passwords</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Readable by all users</td><td>Accessible only by root</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Contains UID, GID, Shell, Home path</td><td>Contains password hashes & expiry policies</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
</details>

<br>

<blockquote>
  💡 <b>Pro Tip:</b> Before modifying or deleting a user account, always verify the user's details using the <code>id username</code> command to prevent accidental changes to the wrong account!
</blockquote>